{# Wikipedia's own summary, ahead of the facts -- something to read before
   the numbers, C15 (#405). Read live from a sample of the cache before
   writing this: no trimming or length gate, because nothing in a real
   sample of cached extracts needed one -- every one read was on-topic,
   factual and city-specific (never a disambiguation opener; `Images`
   already filters those out before they are ever stored). This is a
   full-width prose section, not a card with a fixed height, so the real
   spread seen (182 to 969 characters) is something this layout can carry
   as written rather than something to normalise away.

   Absent silently for the 3 of 233 cities with nothing cached yet or
   nothing Wikipedia had -- the facts grid below still stands on its own,
   the same as before this existed. #}
{% if extract %}
    <p class="place-about">{{ extract }}</p>
{% endif %}

{# Four things somebody checks before booking, and nothing else.

   The timezone is printed from the stored offset rather than the IANA name:
   "GMT+3" answers "how far off is my clock", which is the question, where
   "Europe/Moscow" answers a different one and is longer. Through gmt_offset()
   because the column is a decimal and eight of our cities are on a half hour --
   printing it raw says "GMT+5.5" and rounding it lies to everyone flying to
   India. #}
{% include 'partials/facts-grid.html.twig' with {
    'facts': [
        { 'icon': 'fa-globe', 'value': place.country, 'label': 'Country' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-plane', 'value': place.code, 'label': 'IATA code' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-calendar-days', 'value': place.airports, 'label': 'Airports' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-clock', 'value': gmt_offset(place.timezone), 'label': 'Time zone' }
    ]
} only %}
